// Independently authored staged Loops and Exceptions curriculum.
const completedTopics = <Topic>[
  Topic("python-c2-t0", 2, "while loops and termination", goal:"Trace a repeated decision and prove that a loop reaches its stopping condition.", lesson:"A while loop repeats its body while a condition is truthy. The condition is checked before each iteration, so the body may run zero times. This differs from an if statement: if makes one decision; while returns to the decision after its body finishes.\n\nTrace a loop by recording its state before the condition, the output produced, and its state after the body. In a countdown, a decreasing counter is evidence of progress towards termination. Initialising the counter inside the body would restart that progress. Forgetting to update it can make a loop run indefinitely.\n\nThe last successful condition and the first failed condition are different moments. Starting at three, printing the counter, and decreasing it produces three, two, one. When the counter reaches zero, the condition fails and zero is not printed. Putting the update before the print would change the output.\n\nA loop that depends on user input needs an explicit stopping rule. In our Script runner, provide one response per line in Program input. Exhausting that input is an error, not the same as the user choosing to finish. A later lesson handles conversion errors deliberately.\n\nUse Stop if a program runs indefinitely; the runner's timeout is a safety limit, not a correct termination strategy. Explain why your program stops before declaring it complete.\n\nReflection: What changes on every iteration? What happens when the starting value is zero? Could the condition become false? Compare your predicted trace with the terminal result.", example:"n = 3\nwhile n > 0:\n    print(n)\n    n -= 1\nprint(\"Done\")", output:"3\n2\n1\nDone"),
  Topic("python-c2-t1", 2, "for loops and range", goal:"Choose a bounded iteration and explain inclusive starts and exclusive stops.", lesson:"A for loop takes successive values from an iterable. It does not need a manually updated counter when the intended sequence is already defined. range creates a sequence of integers using a start, an exclusive stop, and optionally a step.\n\nrange(4) supplies 0, 1, 2, 3. range(2, 5) supplies 2, 3, 4. The stop value is excluded. This is useful for counting a known number of repetitions, but it causes off-by-one errors when a question includes an endpoint. To include a positive endpoint n with step one, the stop must be beyond n.\n\nA negative step moves downward. range(3, 0, -1) supplies 3, 2, 1. A step of zero is invalid. A positive step with the start already beyond the stop gives an empty sequence rather than automatically reversing direction.\n\nfor binds its loop variable to each value in turn. Assigning that variable inside the body does not alter range's next value. Choose while when termination depends on changing state; choose for when traversing a known sequence is clearer.\n\nOur projects use repetition to produce several report lines. Each ordinary print call adds a newline. Keep input collection, calculation, and presentation understandable rather than using a long expression solely to reduce line count.\n\nReflection: Write every value in the range before running it. How many iterations occur? Test an empty range, a one-value range, and both endpoints. Explain why the excluded endpoint is not a missing output.", example:"for n in range(2, 5):\n    print(n)", output:"2\n3\n4"),
  Topic("python-c2-t2", 2, "Lists and iteration", goal:"Store ordered records, iterate over values, and distinguish mutation from rebinding.", lesson:"A list holds an ordered collection of objects. Square brackets create a list; commas separate its elements. Indices begin at zero. For a three-element list, index two refers to the final element, while index three is outside the valid range and raises IndexError.\n\nA list can be empty. len gives its element count, not the sum of its values. append adds one object to the end and changes the list in place. It returns None: assigning values = values.append(10) loses your reference to the list and binds values to None instead.\n\nIterate directly when you need values: for minutes in sessions. Use indices only when position matters, and keep their bounds correct. Iterating over an empty list naturally runs the body zero times. Avoid inserting or deleting elements from a list while traversing it unless you deliberately understand the consequences; make a new result list instead.\n\nLists are mutable. If second = first, both names refer to the same list; changing an element through one name is visible through the other. first.copy() creates a new shallow list, although nested mutable objects may still be shared. This differs from the earlier integer reassignment example.\n\nBuild the next project stage by collecting session values into a list, so several calculations can use the same observations. Functions should not unexpectedly change caller-owned lists unless that is their stated purpose.\n\nReflection: Distinguish an empty list, zero as an element, and no valid element at a chosen index. Predict what append returns. Explain whether a filtering function changes its input or returns a separate collection.", example:"sessions = [15, 30, 20]\nfor minutes in sessions:\n    print(minutes)\nprint(len(sessions))", output:"15\n30\n20\n3"),
  Topic("python-c2-t3", 2, "Dictionaries and iteration", goal:"Associate keys with values and aggregate named records without losing duplicates.", lesson:"A dictionary associates keys with values. A list answers questions about sequence positions; a dictionary answers questions about named entries. For example, a subject name can identify a total minute count.\n\nCreate entries with braces and key:value pairs. Looking up an absent key with square brackets raises KeyError. get(key, default) can supply a default instead. Membership with key in mapping tests keys, not values. Dictionary keys are unique: assigning an existing key replaces its value rather than creating a second entry.\n\nIterating directly over a dictionary yields keys. items supplies each key with its associated value, which a loop can unpack into two names. Modern Python preserves insertion order, but a task may require sorted output; never assume insertion order equals alphabetical order.\n\nRepeated records require aggregation. If Python appears twice in a log, assigning its second minute count directly loses the first. Read the accumulated value, add the new observation, and store the result. Choose a default of zero when beginning a numeric total.\n\nDo not change dictionary size while iterating over it. Construct a separate result when filtering. A returned dictionary is useful to a caller; printing it inside the calculation limits reuse.\n\nThe next project stage groups the list of study observations by subject. This connects earlier lists, loops, functions and conditions with a structure suited to named summaries.\n\nReflection: Explain whether your keys are case-sensitive. What happens for repeated subjects, an empty input, or a subject whose total is zero? Test those behaviours explicitly instead of calling all of them missing data.\n\nvalues() exposes the dictionary values; max selects the largest value but fails on an empty collection. sorted returns a new sorted list, which makes tied-name output deterministic.", example:"minutes = {\"Python\": 30, \"Maths\": 20}\nfor subject, value in minutes.items():\n    print(f\"{subject}: {value}\")", output:"Python: 30\nMaths: 20"),
  Topic("python-c2-t4", 2, "Counters and accumulators", goal:"Maintain loop invariants and build a complete multi-record report.", lesson:"A counter records how many qualifying events occurred. An accumulator combines observations, such as total minutes. Initialise them before the loop, otherwise each repetition can erase earlier work.\n\nA useful invariant describes what is already true after every iteration: after processing the first k records, total equals their sum and count equals the number of those records meeting the rule. This helps you explain correctness rather than relying on a few successful outputs.\n\nFor an average, divide the accumulated sum by the count of observations, not by an unrelated target. Specify the empty case before dividing. Maximum calculations also need an empty policy; initialising largest to zero is incorrect if negative observations are permitted. In this chapter, session minutes are nonnegative, but other tasks may differ.\n\nbreak exits the nearest loop. continue skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to its next iteration. In a while loop, placing continue before the state update can prevent progress. Nested loops multiply the work: for each outer value, the inner loop runs again. Trace pairs rather than guessing from indentation.\n\nThe chapter project combines collection, named totals, classification, functions and formatting. Break it into small parts, test each function independently, and integrate them afterwards. A fully working small report is stronger evidence than a large file whose rules you cannot explain.\n\nReflection: State an invariant for your total. What should an empty input produce? Which records contribute to the count? When using a stopping sentinel, should the sentinel itself be counted? Explain why before running tests.\n\nA two-level loop can produce pairs: for row in range(2), and inside it for column in range(3). There are six pairs. break exits only the nearest loop, not every surrounding loop.", example:"values = [10, 30, 40]\ntotal = 0\ncount = 0\nfor value in values:\n    total += value\n    if value >= 30:\n        count += 1\nprint(total)\nprint(count)", output:"80\n2"),
  Topic("python-c3-t0", 3, "Understanding an exception traceback", goal:"Read the failing operation and distinguish parsing errors from runtime exceptions.", lesson:"An exception reports that execution could not complete an operation. A traceback records the active call path leading to the failure. Read its final exception type and message, then inspect the relevant source line and its callers. The highlighted line is where the failure occurred; the incorrect value may have originated earlier.\n\nSyntaxError means Python could not parse the source. IndentationError is a specialised syntax error. Runtime exceptions happen after valid source begins executing: int(\"bad\") raises ValueError, adding an integer to a string can raise TypeError, division by zero raises ZeroDivisionError, and a missing dictionary key raises KeyError.\n\nDo not describe every exception as a device crash. The runner reports failed execution while preserving your saved work. Nor should you change expected output to conceal a traceback: repair the assumption or operation that failed.\n\ntry marks operations from which you intend to recover. except names a matching exception and its recovery action. On an exception, Python skips the rest of that try body and searches for a matching handler. Code after the handled statement can continue. A handler for ValueError does not catch an unrelated TypeError.\n\nKeep the try block small. Wrapping a whole application in a broad except can conceal programming defects and falsely report success. Catch the failure that the task actually expects. The lesson deliberately demonstrates a failure before later exercises build recovery.\n\nReflection: State the exception type, failing expression and bad input separately. Can you reproduce the failure with one small example? Explain which assumption was violated, then test a valid value as well as the failing value.", example:"try:\n    int(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Invalid integer text\")\nprint(\"Continued\")", output:"Invalid integer text\nContinued"),
  Topic("python-c3-t1", 3, "Recovering from invalid numeric input", goal:"Combine narrow recovery, value validation and a terminating retry loop.", lesson:"Conversion asks whether text can represent a number. Validation asks whether the resulting number belongs to the permitted domain. int(\"-5\") succeeds, but negative minutes may still be unacceptable. Treat the two stages separately so feedback describes the actual issue.\n\nAn input loop can retry after a recognised failure. Each iteration must consume a new response; repeating a conversion of the same unchanged bad string never makes progress. Use a clear exit policy such as a valid value, a cancel command, or a bounded number of attempts.\n\nexcept handles a matching error from the try body. else runs when that body finishes without an exception. finally runs when leaving the try statement, including many error paths, and is useful for cleanup rather than as a success indicator. Do not return from finally merely to suppress a failure.\n\nThe supplied Program input box contains one response per line. A retrying exercise may need several lines. Running out of supplied input is not automatically a valid number or a cancellation. The activity must state what to do in that case.\n\nFor reusable functions, returning a result is more useful than asking for input internally. Keep interactive retry logic in the interface and independently check conversion/validation functions. Preserve earlier successful records when a later input fails.\n\nReflection: Try bad text, a well-formed but negative number, zero, and a positive value. Explain which are conversion failures and which are domain decisions. Does your loop consume new input and have a definite stopping path? What does the user see when cancelling?\n\nA loop may have an else clause: it runs if the loop finishes normally without break. A break skips that loop else. This differs from the else belonging to an if or try; indentation identifies which statement owns it.", example:"for text in [\"bad\", \"-2\", \"10\"]:\n    try:\n        minutes = int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"Not an integer\")\n        continue\n    if minutes < 0:\n        print(\"Negative minutes\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"Accepted: {minutes}\")", output:"Not an integer\nNegative minutes\nAccepted: 10"),
  Topic("python-c3-t2", 3, "Raising and propagating errors", goal:"Specify function contracts, raise meaningful errors and keep recovery at the right layer.", lesson:"A function contract describes its accepted inputs, result and failure behaviour. Sometimes invalid input should cause an exception rather than a default value. Returning zero for an invalid target could silently turn a broken report into an apparently valid result.\n\nraise ValueError(\"Target must be positive\") communicates that a supplied value violates the contract. The caller may catch the exception and decide how to inform the user. This separates calculation from interface decisions.\n\nAn exception not handled in a function propagates to its caller. If no caller handles it, execution ends with a traceback. Propagation is useful: a small function should not conceal every possible failure. Use specific handlers at the layer that can genuinely recover.\n\nUse bare raise inside an exception handler to re-raise the active exception when you cannot resolve it. Do not use a broad handler that prints success after arbitrary failures. Catching BaseException also intercepts interruption-related exceptions and is rarely appropriate for ordinary input handling.\n\nType and value requirements are distinct. For our target function, require actual integers; bool is a subclass of int in Python, so type(value) is int excludes True and False when the contract explicitly says integers only. Values must then satisfy the permitted range.\n\nYour completed project integrates named session records from Loops, explicit validation, percentage calculation and a text report. Failed records must be counted honestly. A successful test checks behaviour, not whether source text matches a reference. Explain the contract and retry policy in My Notes.\n\nReflection: When would None be a legitimate missing result, and when should an error be raised? Which layer can decide whether to retry? Test valid inputs, invalid values and invalid types separately.", example:"def percentage(total, target):\n    if target <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Target must be positive\")\n    return total / target * 100\n\ntry:\n    percentage(30, 0)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)", output:"Target must be positive"),
];
